window.docContents[78].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Initial-value-and-classical-solutions":{"contents":"Initial-value data consist of a force , a prescribed velocity , and\ndistributional incompressibility of . A finite-time residual solution\nstores , , a positive horizon , the Euler momentum equation and\npointwise incompressibility on , and the exact value\n.A residual solution carries no differentiability. A smooth solution also\nrequires ambient smoothness at every positive-time\npoint and one-sided continuity of velocity at . A global smooth solution\nhas these properties at all positive times and relative smoothness on\n. Every global smooth solution restricts, with\nthe same fields and data, to every positive finite horizon. Smoothness alone\non the whole space does not imply the  bounds, decay, and spacetime\nintegrability required by a weak solution, so a smooth solution is weak only\nonce those bounds are proved.\n\nFor zero initial velocity and zero forcing,  and  give a smooth\nEuler solution globally and on every positive finite horizon.\n\nEvery time derivative, convective term, pressure gradient, and divergence is\nzero. Constant zero fields are smooth on the closed physical half-space, and\ntheir initial value is the prescribed zero datum. Restricting the global\nsolution gives the finite-time result.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.1. Initial-value and classical solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Initial-value-and-classical-solutions"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--A-Quadratic-Integral-Model":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.6. A Quadratic Integral Model","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--A-Quadratic-Integral-Model"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Operational-domain-geometry":{"contents":"First-order extension, trace, chart localization, Morrey transport, and\nRellich compactness use the same\nfinite extension atlas.\nIt supplies the boundary cover, charts, flattening maps,\ncutoffs, Jacobian bounds, derivative bounds, and overlap data consumed by the\nanalytic proofs.\n\nFor a bounded open set with local  graph boundary, the geometric\nconstruction supplies this atlas: local graph flattening gives the charts,\ncompactness gives a finite cover, and a smooth partition assembles their\ncutoffs. We keep the atlas in the analytic interface so that successive\ntheorems can use the same charts and refer to their quantitative bounds.\n\nConcrete instances include:\n\n* the interval , by affine endpoint charts;* every positive-dimensional Euclidean unit ball, by signed caps and explicit\n  graph flattening;* a  atlas for the unit ball, which supplies the inherited \n  extension atlas needed by first-order theory.\n\nA supplied extension of any order can also be multiplied by a smooth cutoff\nto obtain support in any prescribed open neighborhood of ,\nwhich gives a new bounded extension of the same order.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.3. Operational domain geometry","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Operational-domain-geometry"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Rellich___Kondrachov-compactness":{"contents":"On an operational bounded  domain, the first-order embedding\n\n\n\nis compact throughout the strict subcritical range formalized in the\ncompactness chapter, including the non-Hilbert endpoint\n.  The proof separates:\n\n1. translation control from weak derivatives;2. tightness from bounded support after extension and cutoff;3. the Fréchet–Kolmogorov compactness criterion;4. interpolation and finite-measure exponent changes;5. restriction back to the original domain.\n\nCompactness is proved in the strict range ; at  the embedding\nis continuous but, classically, not compact.\n\nThe higher-order theory lowers derivative order coordinatewise and gives a\ncompact operator between graph spaces where the exponent and order\nconditions are available.  Evolution compactness of Aubin–Lions type also\nneeds control of time derivatives and belongs to the parabolic theory.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.8. Rellich–Kondrachov compactness","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Rellich___Kondrachov-compactness"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--Geometric-hypotheses":{"contents":"The geometric assumptions differ by theorem family.\n\n* Whole-space Sobolev and Morrey estimates require no boundary geometry.* Domain Sobolev and Morrey estimates use either an explicit extension\n  operator or the operational bounded  atlas.* In positive dimension, the completed Poincaré theorem needs only a bounded\n  set, because its source is already the zero-boundary closure.* Completed finite- Hardy quotients are available on the positive\n  half-line and the flat upper half-space without further geometry, and on a\n  general domain through the operational atlas.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Sobolev, Morrey, Poincaré, and Hardy Inequalities","header":"4.3.4.3. Geometric hypotheses","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--Geometric-hypotheses"}});